# ktalk

`ktalk` is a CLI over the Kontur.Talk API. Commands are grouped by API tag; every command prints JSON.

## Read the help before typing a command

The groups are guessable; the subcommands and flags are not. Read the help first — it is instant and
needs no credentials:

```bash
ktalk --help                     # every group
ktalk <group> --help             # a group's subcommands
ktalk <group> <command> --help   # a command's flags, before first use
```

The help outranks this file: where they disagree, the help is current and this is stale.

## Go through the CLI

Do not `curl` the API or hand-roll a client — the CLI handles the `X-Auth-Token` header, `429`
back-off (add no `sleep`/retry loops), cursor pagination, and mixed date formats. Companions are `jq`
and shell. If no command exists for the task, say so rather than improvising a raw call.

## Credentials and scopes

Two environment variables (no config file); `--base-url` / `--token` override per command:

```bash
export KTALK_BASE_URL="https://<space>.ktalk.ru"
export KTALK_TOKEN="<x-auth-token>"   # admin panel → API keys; never echo it
```

Keys are scoped: a read-only key returns `Forbidden` (403) on writes. `ktalk api-keys access-info`
shows what it allows. A rejected request fails the same way twice — report it, don't retry around it.

## Keys, pagination, transcripts

- List to find keys (`ktalk recordings list`, `ktalk users search --query …`); match a recording on
  `title`/`createdDate` with `jq` rather than guessing.
- `recordings list` is cursor-paginated (`{items, nextPageToken}`); pass `--all` to follow pages.
- A recording's artifacts are transcript, summary, and media file; `recordings transcript … --format
  text` renders a readable speaker dialogue. Write commands take `--from-json <path>`.
- Don't infer a fact from a missing field — fetch the endpoint that carries it.

Dates are ISO 8601. `delete` / `end-conference` / `revoke-sessions` act immediately — confirm first.
If `ktalk` is missing, install via mise (`mise use github:AllDmeat/ktalk`) or from
[Releases](https://github.com/AllDmeat/ktalk/releases).
